#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""keydrop — hand a secret to a local tool or agent through a one-shot link
instead of pasting it into a chat.

    keydrop request <name> [--type secret|credential|<template>] [--fields totp,url,notes]
                   [--note "what it's for"] [--ttl 15m] [--http]
    keydrop vault [--ttl 15m] [--http]
                              a session link for listing, adding, replacing
                              and deleting entries under the service prefix
    keydrop get <name> [--field F]
                              print the stored value, or one credential field
    keydrop list [--long]     names only; --long adds type, created, updated
    keydrop status            pending requests and vault sessions
    keydrop cancel <name>     stop a pending request (`cancel vault` ends a session)
    keydrop home              the vault page held up for good, for launchd
                              (HTTPS only: no --http)
    keydrop home --new-link
                              mint the home page's link (prints it once)
    keydrop home --stop       kill switch: take the home page down and keep it down
    keydrop home --unlock     clear a lockout or a stop
                              (--new-link and --unlock refuse while any KEYDROP_*
                              env var is set unless given --force; --stop warns)
    keydrop config            print the settings in effect (never a value)

`request` binds a one-shot HTTP server to 127.0.0.1, exposes it on the tailnet
with `tailscale serve` on the same port number, prints the URL and detaches. The
page proposes a single secret or validated credential fields. One good submit, or the TTL, or a handful of
bad guesses, and it tears its serve config down and exits.

The vault is the login keychain: service `<prefix><name>` (prefix `keydrop.`
by default), account from the config (`keydrop` by default).
Created/updated times are the keychain item's own cdat/mdat. Typed fields are
stored together in the spec v1 JSON envelope, with a non-secret label marker.
Field definitions and rotate-by dates live in items.json, never field values. Notes live in
meta.json in the state dir, because a note isn't a secret and `security -i`
has no quoting that's safe for free text; values never go there.

`vault` is `request` held open as a session: same token-in-path link on its own
tailnet port, same TTL cap, lockout and teardown, but it stays up until the
TTL or the Done button. The page is write-only. It lists names, notes and
times and takes new values; it never renders a stored value, so nothing that
reads the page (a screenshot, the browser's cache, someone over a shoulder)
can see one. Every POST needs the session's form token as well as the path
token. Delete asks to confirm first, and can only ever reach a
`<prefix><NAME_RE>` item with the configured account.

On the tailnet, requests must carry `Tailscale-User-Login: <owner login>`, the
owner's tailnet login from the config. There is no default: without one, no
page starts.
`tailscale serve` deletes any incoming Tailscale-User-* header and sets its own
from WhoIs on the connection (ipn/ipnlocal/serve.go, addTailscaleIdentityHeaders),
so a tailnet client can't forge it. It is not a defence against a process on
this Mac: if the Mac is logged in to the tailnet as the owner, its own traffic
through serve carries the owner's login, and anything that connects to
127.0.0.1 directly can send
any header it likes. Those still need the token. An underscore spelling
(Tailscale_User_Login) survives serve untouched, so any header that
normalises to the login header's name, other than exactly one real one, is
refused.

Where the secret goes, and where it must never go:

- The write is `security -i` with the command on stdin and the value as hex
  (`-X`). `security add-generic-password -w <value>` would put it on argv, which
  any process can read with `ps`. Using `security` rather than calling the
  Security framework from Python also matters for `get`: an item's ACL trusts
  the binary that created it, so a Python-created item would pop a keychain
  dialog every time `security find-generic-password` read it.
- Nothing is logged but name, time and outcome. The http.server request log is
  switched off, because its default line is the request path, and the path is
  the token.
- The token is never written to the state dir either. It's a capability, not
  the secret, but whoever holds it decides what ends up under that name.

Serve uses a dedicated tailnet port per request rather than a path on :443, so
the token never lands in tailscaled's serve config (which it persists to disk)
and teardown can't touch anyone else's routes. Never `funnel`: the page must
not be reachable from the internet.

The tailnet port is always the loopback port, and the order is bind, add the
serve route, then listen. This was worked out against tailscaled running
with userspace networking (netstack); a kernel-TUN tailscaled was not
analysed. Under netstack, tailscaled forwards inbound tailnet TCP for the node's own IP to
127.0.0.1:<same port> unless a serve handler claims that port
(wgengine/netstack/netstack.go, Impl.acceptTCP: lb.TCPHandlerForDst first,
the 127.0.0.1 rewrite and forwardTCP only after; ipn/ipnlocal/netstack.go,
LocalBackend.TCPHandlerForDst, reaches tcpHandlerForServe in
ipn/ipnlocal/serve.go, which answers for any port in the serve config). So a
loopback listener on a port serve isn't claiming is open to every tailnet
peer, with any headers it likes. Sharing the number means the port is claimed
for as long as the route is up; not listening until the route exists means
there is no window where it isn't (a bound socket that isn't listening drops
the SYN). claim_port picks a loopback port serve isn't using, or refuses.
There is no --local on any page, for the same reason: a loopback listener with
no route in front is open to every tailnet peer.

The route can still go away while a page is up (someone runs `serve reset`,
or repoints the port). From then on netstack forwards peers straight to the
listener with whatever Tailscale-User-Login they like, so every request, on
every page, first reads `serve status` and checks the route is still a
single `/` proxy to 127.0.0.1:<this port> (route_ours). If it isn't, or the
status can't be read or parsed, the request gets a bare 404 and the page ends
with outcome route-gone: the serve loop stops as soon as that response is
written, the listener closes, and the process exits (home exits 1). Failing
closed matters: "can't read the config" is not evidence the route is there.
Teardown then leaves the port alone if serve shows it gone or pointing
somewhere else; it only removes a route that is still ours.

Teardown runs on every exit path the process can see: submit, TTL, lockout,
SIGTERM/SIGINT/SIGHUP, and exceptions. SIGKILL can't be caught, so `request`
and `status` also reap pending records whose process is gone. A teardown only
counts once `serve status` shows the port gone; until then the record stays,
marked teardown-failed, and every reap retries it. Otherwise a teardown that
ran while tailscaled was down would leave the route in tailscaled's persisted
config with nothing left to remove it. Reap also sweeps orphans, but only
routes in keydrop's own ledger (routes.json in the state dir: an entry is
written before `serve` runs and dropped once the route is confirmed gone)
that no record owns and that still point where keydrop pointed them. A route
keydrop has no ledger entry for is never removed, even one shaped exactly
like keydrop's (see orphan_routes).

`home` is the vault page with no TTL, meant to run under launchd on its own
tailnet port (HOME_PORT). It reuses the vault handler and adds what the TTL
used to provide: bad requests (5 in 24h) lock it out, and the lockout is
written to the state dir, so a restart doesn't clear it; adds, replaces and
deletes are rate limited (also persisted); every write attempt goes to
home-audit.log (time, name, action, outcome; never a value); and `home
--stop`, or the page's own Stop button, tears the serve route down and leaves
a flag that keeps it down until `home --unlock`. The path token stays, minted
by `home --new-link`, which prints it once; only its SHA-256 is kept. A bad
request counts toward the lockout only if it carries the owner's login and
the browser's own Sec-Fetch-Site says same-origin or none; anything else (a
cross-site request, an <img> on some page, a request with no Fetch Metadata)
gets a bare 404 and no strike, so a web page the owner happens to visit can't
lock them out of their own page. Browsers send no Fetch Metadata over plain HTTP, so
home is HTTPS only and refuses --http. It binds 127.0.0.1:HOME_PORT, the
port its route claims. Besides the per-request route check, it reads `serve
status` every WATCH_INTERVAL seconds while idle, so a page nobody is using
doesn't sit on a port netstack would now forward to; the request check is the
guarantee, the watchdog only shortens how long an idle page lingers. The per-minute request limit is charged
only after the login check, so another tailnet node can't spend it. Every
page (request, vault and home) answers only an exact Host allowlist (its
tailnet name, with and without the tailnet port, and 127.0.0.1/localhost on
its own loopback port), so a DNS-rebinding page in a browser on the Mac gets
a bare 404 before the login pin is even looked at.

Configuration comes from a JSON file, ~/.config/keydrop/config.json (or the
path in KEYDROP_CONFIG), and environment variables, which win over the file:

    owner_login      KEYDROP_OWNER_LOGIN     required for request, vault, home
    account          KEYDROP_ACCOUNT         keychain account (default keydrop)
    service_prefix   KEYDROP_SERVICE_PREFIX  keychain service prefix (default keydrop.)
    state_dir        KEYDROP_STATE_DIR       default ~/.local/state/keydrop
    launchd_label    KEYDROP_LAUNCHD_LABEL   default local.keydrop.home
    home_port        KEYDROP_HOME_PORT       default 9443
    tailscale        KEYDROP_TAILSCALE       default: tailscale on PATH
    tailscale_socket KEYDROP_TS_SOCKET       passed as --socket only when set

Test-only overrides: KEYDROP_SECURITY (the `security` binary) and
KEYDROP_WATCH_INTERVAL (seconds, capped at WATCH_INTERVAL).
"""
import base64, calendar, fcntl, hashlib, hmac, html, http.server, json, os, re, secrets, shutil
import signal, subprocess, sys, time, urllib.parse

CONFIG_PATH = os.environ.get("KEYDROP_CONFIG") or os.path.expanduser("~/.config/keydrop/config.json")
CONFIG_KEYS = {   # file key: (env var, default)
    "owner_login": ("KEYDROP_OWNER_LOGIN", ""),
    "account": ("KEYDROP_ACCOUNT", "keydrop"),
    "service_prefix": ("KEYDROP_SERVICE_PREFIX", "keydrop."),
    "state_dir": ("KEYDROP_STATE_DIR", "~/.local/state/keydrop"),
    "launchd_label": ("KEYDROP_LAUNCHD_LABEL", "local.keydrop.home"),
    "home_port": ("KEYDROP_HOME_PORT", "9443"),
    "tailscale": ("KEYDROP_TAILSCALE", ""),
    "tailscale_socket": ("KEYDROP_TS_SOCKET", ""),
}
# These end up inside the `security -i` command line, which splits on
# whitespace and honours quotes, so they are held to a plain charset.
ACCOUNT_RE = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9._@+-]{1,64}")
PREFIX_RE = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,64}")
LABEL_RE = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,128}")
# A Tailscale login as serve sends it: user@domain, or user@github and the like.
LOGIN_RE = re.compile(r"[^\s@\x00-\x1f\x7f]+@[^\s@\x00-\x1f\x7f]+")


def load_config():
    """(settings, problems). Env vars win over the file; a missing file is
    fine, an unreadable or malformed one is a problem, never a silent default.
    Each problem is (key, message); key is None for the file as a whole."""
    problems, data = [], {}
    try:
        with open(CONFIG_PATH) as f:
            data = json.load(f)
        if not isinstance(data, dict):
            problems.append((None, f"{CONFIG_PATH}: not a JSON object"))
            data = {}
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
        problems.append((None, f"{CONFIG_PATH}: {e}"))
    unknown = sorted(set(data) - set(CONFIG_KEYS))
    if unknown:
        # A typo'd key (state-dir) silently means the default: never ignorable.
        problems.append((None, f"{CONFIG_PATH}: unknown keys {', '.join(unknown)}"))
    out = {}
    for key, (env, default) in CONFIG_KEYS.items():
        val = os.environ.get(env)
        if val is None:
            val = data.get(key, default)
        out[key] = str(val).strip() if val is not None else ""
    out["owner_login"] = out["owner_login"].lower()
    if out["owner_login"] and not LOGIN_RE.fullmatch(out["owner_login"]):
        problems.append(("owner_login", f"owner_login {out['owner_login']!r} doesn't look like a tailnet login (user@domain)"))
    if not ACCOUNT_RE.fullmatch(out["account"]):
        problems.append(("account", "account must be 1-64 of A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ @ + -"))
    if not PREFIX_RE.fullmatch(out["service_prefix"]):
        problems.append(("service_prefix", "service_prefix must be 1-64 of A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ -"))
    if not LABEL_RE.fullmatch(out["launchd_label"]):
        problems.append(("launchd_label", "launchd_label must be 1-128 of A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ -"))
    try:
        port = int(out["home_port"])
        if not 1 <= port <= 65535:
            raise ValueError
    except ValueError:
        problems.append(("home_port", f"home_port {out['home_port']!r} is not a port number"))
        port = 9443
    out["home_port"] = port
    if not out["state_dir"]:
        problems.append(("state_dir", "state_dir is empty"))
        out["state_dir"] = CONFIG_KEYS["state_dir"][1]
    out["state_dir"] = os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(out["state_dir"]))
    return out, problems


CONFIG, _PROBLEMS = load_config()
CONFIG_PROBLEMS = [m for _, m in _PROBLEMS]
# What `home --stop` can't run past: anything that might point it at the wrong
# state dir or port. A bad owner_login or account shouldn't block the kill switch.
STOP_PROBLEMS = [m for k, m in _PROBLEMS if k in (None, "state_dir", "home_port")]
STATE_DIR = CONFIG["state_dir"]
PENDING_DIR = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "pending")
LOG_FILE = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "keydrop.log")
META_FILE = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "meta.json")
ROUTES_FILE = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "routes.json")   # the ledger: every route keydrop made
TS_SOCKET = os.path.expanduser(CONFIG["tailscale_socket"]) if CONFIG["tailscale_socket"] else ""
TAILSCALE = CONFIG["tailscale"] or shutil.which("tailscale") or "/opt/homebrew/bin/tailscale"
SECURITY = os.environ.get("KEYDROP_SECURITY", "/usr/bin/security")

SERVICE_PREFIX = CONFIG["service_prefix"]
ACCOUNT = CONFIG["account"]
NAME_RE = re.compile(r"^[a-z0-9._-]{1,64}$")
NOTE_MAX = 200
DEFAULT_TTL = 15 * 60
MAX_TTL = 60 * 60
MAX_BAD = 5            # wrong-token requests before the server gives up
MAX_REQUESTS = 50      # any requests at all, so a scanner can't keep it busy
MAX_SESSION_REQUESTS = 400   # the same cap for a vault session, which is used for longer
OWNER_LOGIN = CONFIG["owner_login"]   # "" until configured: then no page starts
LAUNCHD_LABEL = CONFIG["launchd_label"]
LOGIN_HEADER = "Tailscale-User-Login"
VAULT = "[vault]"      # a vault session's record/log name; never a valid key name
HOME = "[home]"        # the home page's record/log name
HOME_PORT = CONFIG["home_port"]   # tailnet and loopback both
WATCH_INTERVAL = 10    # seconds between home's checks that its serve route is still there
HOME_FILE = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "home.json")
AUDIT_FILE = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "home-audit.log")
HOME_BAD_WINDOW = 24 * 3600   # MAX_BAD bad requests inside this, and home locks until --unlock
WRITE_LIMIT = 10              # adds/replaces/deletes per WRITE_WINDOW on the home page
WRITE_WINDOW = 3600
REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE = 60      # any requests at all; past this, a bare 429
CSRF_MAX_AGE = 12 * 3600      # a home form older than this asks for a reload
MAX_BODY = 16 * 1024
REQUEST_TIMEOUT = 10   # per-connection socket timeout; one slow client can't hold the link
SERVE_TIMEOUT = 20     # `tailscale serve` blocks forever if serve isn't enabled
PORT_PICK_TRIES = 32   # loopback ports tried before claim_port gives up
# Paths browsers fetch on their own. 404'd, but not counted as guesses.
AUTO_FETCH = ("/favicon", "/apple-touch-icon", "/robots.txt")


class KeydropError(Exception):
    exit_code = 1


class RefusedError(KeydropError):
    """A refused request (SPEC 6): exit status 2."""
    exit_code = 2


class PolicyError(RefusedError):
    pass


def require_config(stop=False):
    problems = STOP_PROBLEMS if stop else CONFIG_PROBLEMS
    if problems:
        raise KeydropError("config: " + "; ".join(problems))


def require_owner():
    """Every page pins the owner's login. With none configured there is
    nothing to pin to, so nothing serves: never fall back to open."""
    require_config()
    if not OWNER_LOGIN:
        raise KeydropError(f"no owner login configured. Set owner_login in {CONFIG_PATH} "
                           "(or KEYDROP_OWNER_LOGIN) to the tailnet login allowed to submit values, "
                           "e.g. you@example.com. Confirm it yourself: `tailscale status --json` "
                           "(under User) or the admin console are places to look, but it's your call "
                           "which login that is")


# ---------- validation ----------

def valid_name(name):
    # fullmatch, not match: `$` also matches before a trailing newline, and a
    # newline in a name would end the `security -i` command line early.
    return bool(NAME_RE.fullmatch(name or ""))


def parse_ttl(text):
    m = re.fullmatch(r"(\d+)([smh]?)", (text or "").strip())
    if not m:
        raise KeydropError(f"bad --ttl {text!r} (use e.g. 90s, 15m, 1h)")
    secs = int(m.group(1)) * {"": 1, "s": 1, "m": 60, "h": 3600}[m.group(2)]
    if not 1 <= secs <= MAX_TTL:
        raise KeydropError(f"--ttl must be between 1s and {MAX_TTL // 60}m")
    return secs


def clean_note(note):
    note = re.sub(r"[\x00-\x1f\x7f]", " ", note or "").strip()
    if len(note) > NOTE_MAX:
        raise KeydropError(f"--note is limited to {NOTE_MAX} characters")
    return note


# ---------- typed items (definitions only; never field values in state) ----------

ITEMS_FILE = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "items.json")
POLICY_FILE = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "policy.json")
FIELD_LIMIT = 12


def schema_field(name, kind="secret", required=True, label=None, max=8192, check=None):
    return {"name": name, "label": label or name.replace("_", " ").capitalize(),
            "kind": kind, "required": required, "max": max, **({"check": check} if check else {})}


# SPEC 5.3. The optional fields are shown only when asked for with
# --fields totp,url,notes, and policy can forbid each one.
CREDENTIAL_FIELDS = [
    schema_field("username", "text", True, "Username", 512),
    schema_field("password", "secret", True, "Password"),
    schema_field("totp", "secret", False, "One-time code secret", 1024, "totp"),
    schema_field("url", "url", False, "Website", 2048),
    schema_field("notes", "secret", False, "Notes", 2000),
]
CREDENTIAL_OPTIONAL = ("totp", "url", "notes")


def credential_schema(name, optional=()):
    unknown = set(optional) - set(CREDENTIAL_OPTIONAL)
    if unknown:
        raise RefusedError(f"unknown credential field {sorted(unknown)[0]!r} "
                           f"(choose from {','.join(CREDENTIAL_OPTIONAL)})")
    return validate_schema({"service": name, "fields": [
        f for f in CREDENTIAL_FIELDS if f["required"] or f["name"] in optional]})


TEMPLATES = {
    "api-key": [schema_field("api_key")],
    "username-password": [schema_field("username", "text"), schema_field("password")],
    "linkedin": [schema_field("client_id", "text"), schema_field("client_secret"), schema_field("redirect_uri", "url")],
    "github": [schema_field("token")], "openai": [schema_field("api_key")],
    "anthropic": [schema_field("api_key")],
    "aws": [schema_field("access_key_id", "text"), schema_field("secret_access_key"), schema_field("session_token", required=False)],
    "google-oauth": [schema_field("client_id", "text"), schema_field("client_secret"), schema_field("redirect_uri", "url")],
    "stripe": [schema_field("secret_key"), schema_field("publishable_key", "text", False)],
    "credential": [f for f in CREDENTIAL_FIELDS if f["required"]],
}


def schema_json(raw):
    if len(raw.encode("utf-8", "surrogatepass")) > 16384:
        raise ValueError("schema is too large")
    def unique(pairs):
        result = {}
        for key, value in pairs:
            if key in result:
                raise ValueError("duplicate schema property")
            result[key] = value
        return result
    try:
        return json.loads(raw, object_pairs_hook=unique)
    except RecursionError:
        raise ValueError("schema nesting is too deep")


def safe_label(value, limit=80):
    return (isinstance(value, str) and 0 < len(value) <= limit
            and value.isprintable() and value.strip() != "" and not re.search(r"[<>]", value))


def validate_schema(data):
    """Fail the entire untrusted definition; never accept defaults/values/code."""
    fail = RefusedError("Invalid credential schema.")
    if not isinstance(data, dict) or set(data) != {"service", "fields"}:
        raise fail
    if not isinstance(data["service"], str) or not valid_name(data["service"]):
        raise fail
    fs = data["fields"]
    if not isinstance(fs, list) or not 1 <= len(fs) <= FIELD_LIMIT:
        raise fail
    seen, result = set(), []
    for f in fs:
        if not isinstance(f, dict) or set(f) - {"name", "id", "label", "kind", "required", "max", "options", "check"}:
            raise fail
        if "check" in f and (f["check"] != "totp" or f.get("kind") != "secret"):
            raise fail
        name = f.get("name", f.get("id"))
        if ("name" in f and "id" in f or not isinstance(name, str)
                or not re.fullmatch(r"[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,31}", name) or name in seen
                or not safe_label(f.get("label")) or f.get("kind") not in ("secret", "text", "url", "select")
                or type(f.get("required")) is not bool
                or type(f.get("max", 8192)) is not int or not 1 <= f.get("max", 8192) <= 8192):
            raise fail
        opts = f.get("options")
        if f["kind"] == "select":
            if (not isinstance(opts, list) or not 1 <= len(opts) <= 20
                    or not all(safe_label(v) for v in opts) or len(set(opts)) != len(opts)):
                raise fail
        elif opts is not None:
            raise fail
        seen.add(name)
        result.append(dict(name=name, label=f["label"], kind=f["kind"], required=f["required"],
                           max=f.get("max", 8192), **({"options": opts} if opts is not None else {}),
                           **({"check": f["check"]} if "check" in f else {})))
    if not any(f["kind"] == "secret" for f in result):
        raise fail
    normalized = {"service": data["service"], "fields": result}
    if len(json.dumps(normalized, ensure_ascii=False).encode("utf-8")) > 8192:
        raise fail
    return normalized


def template_schema(service):
    if service not in TEMPLATES:
        raise RefusedError("Unknown template. Supply --schema with the service requirements.")
    return validate_schema({"service": service, "fields": TEMPLATES[service]})


def read_policy():
    """SPEC 5.5. No policy file means secret-only; credentials are opt-in.
    credential_fields_enabled defaults to every optional credential field."""
    try:
        with open(POLICY_FILE) as f:
            policy = json.load(f)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        policy = {}
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        raise PolicyError("can't read the item policy")
    if not isinstance(policy, dict):
        raise PolicyError("can't read the item policy")
    types = policy.get("types_enabled", ["secret"])
    fields = policy.get("credential_fields_enabled", list(CREDENTIAL_OPTIONAL))
    if not (isinstance(types, list) and isinstance(fields, list)
            and all(isinstance(x, str) for x in types + fields)):
        raise PolicyError("can't read the item policy")
    return {"types_enabled": types, "credential_fields_enabled": fields}


def check_policy(schema):
    policy = read_policy()
    typ = "credential" if schema else "secret"
    if typ not in policy["types_enabled"]:
        raise PolicyError(f"{typ} requests are turned off on this machine")
    for f in (schema or {}).get("fields", []):
        if f["name"] in CREDENTIAL_OPTIONAL and f["name"] not in policy["credential_fields_enabled"]:
            raise PolicyError(f"credential requests with a {f['name']} field are turned off on this machine")


def credentials_enabled():
    try:
        check_policy(template_schema("api-key"))
        return True
    except KeydropError:
        return False


def read_items():
    try:
        with open(ITEMS_FILE) as f:
            data = json.load(f)
        if not isinstance(data, dict):
            return {}
        result = {}
        for name, item in data.items():
            if not valid_name(name) or not isinstance(item, dict):
                continue
            schema = validate_schema(item["schema"]) if item.get("schema") else None
            date = rotation_date(item.get("rotate_by", ""))
            result[name] = {"schema": schema, "rotate_by": date}
        return result
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeydropError, TypeError):
        return {}


def write_item_meta(name, schema, rotate_by):
    with meta_lock():
        data = read_items()
        if schema is None and rotate_by is None:
            data.pop(name, None)
        else:
            data[name] = {"schema": schema, "rotate_by": rotate_by or ""}
        fd = os.open(ITEMS_FILE + ".tmp", os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, 0o600)
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as f:
            json.dump(data, f)
        os.replace(ITEMS_FILE + ".tmp", ITEMS_FILE)


def rotation_date(value):
    if not isinstance(value, str):
        raise KeydropError("Use a valid rotate-by date.")
    if value:
        try:
            if not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}", value):
                raise ValueError()
            time.strptime(value, "%Y-%m-%d")
        except ValueError:
            raise KeydropError("Use a valid rotate-by date.")
    return value


TOO_LONG = "That's too long to store in this vault."


def submitted_item(fields, fallback=None):
    if any(len(v) != 1 for v in fields.values()):
        raise KeydropError("That didn't look like the form.")
    raw = field(fields, "schema")
    try:
        schema = validate_schema(schema_json(raw)) if raw else fallback
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        raise KeydropError("Invalid credential schema.")
    check_policy(schema)
    if not schema:
        if set(fields) - {"csrf", "action", "name", "note", "rotate_by", "value", "schema"}:
            raise KeydropError("That didn't look like the form.")
        value = field(fields, "value").strip()
        if not value:
            raise KeydropError("Nothing was entered.")
        if len(value.encode()) > 8192:
            raise KeydropError("Value is too long.")
        return value, None
    schema = validate_schema(schema)
    allowed = {"csrf", "action", "name", "note", "schema", "rotate_by"} | {"f_" + f["name"] for f in schema["fields"]}
    if set(fields) - allowed:
        raise KeydropError("That didn't look like the form.")
    values = {}
    for f in schema["fields"]:
        v = field(fields, "f_" + f["name"])
        if f["required"] and not v.strip():
            raise KeydropError("Nothing was entered.")
        if len(v.encode()) > f["max"]:
            raise KeydropError("A field is too long.")
        if v and f["kind"] == "url":
            try:
                url = urllib.parse.urlsplit(v)
            except ValueError:
                raise KeydropError("A URL doesn't look right.")
            if url.scheme not in ("http", "https") or not url.netloc or re.search(r"[\x00-\x20]", v):
                raise KeydropError("A URL doesn't look right.")
        if v and f.get("check") == "totp" and not totp_ok(v):
            raise KeydropError("The one-time code secret doesn't look right.")
        if v and f["kind"] == "select" and v not in f["options"]:
            raise KeydropError("Choose a listed option.")
        if v:
            values[f["name"]] = v
    return json.dumps({"keydrop": 1, "type": "credential", "fields": values}, ensure_ascii=False), schema


def totp_ok(value):
    """base32, or an otpauth:// URI carrying a base32 secret (SPEC 5.3)."""
    if value.startswith("otpauth://"):
        try:
            url = urllib.parse.urlsplit(value)
            secret = urllib.parse.parse_qs(url.query).get("secret", [""])
        except ValueError:
            return False
        if url.netloc not in ("totp", "hotp") or len(secret) != 1:
            return False
        value = secret[0]
    b32 = re.sub(r"[\s-]", "", value).upper().rstrip("=")
    if not re.fullmatch(r"[A-Z2-7]{16,}", b32):
        return False
    try:
        base64.b32decode(b32 + "=" * (-len(b32) % 8))
        return True
    except ValueError:
        return False


def item_value(name, raw, selected=None):
    # State marks envelopes we wrote: a legacy secret that resembles JSON stays raw.
    entry = STORE.list().get(name)
    if entry is None:
        raise KeydropError("Cannot establish the item type.")
    typed = entry.get("type") == "credential"
    schema = read_items().get(name, {}).get("schema")
    if not schema and not typed:
        if selected not in (None, "value"):
            raise KeydropError("Unknown field.")
        return raw
    if not selected:
        raise KeydropError("Credentials require get <name> --field <name>.")
    try:
        data = json.loads(raw)
        if data.get("keydrop") != 1 or data.get("type") != "credential" or not isinstance(data.get("fields"), dict):
            raise ValueError()
        value = data["fields"][selected]
        if not isinstance(value, str):
            raise ValueError()
        return value
    except (ValueError, KeyError, TypeError, AttributeError):
        raise KeydropError("Field is absent or the item is invalid.")


# ---------- keychain ----------

def service(name):
    return SERVICE_PREFIX + name


# `security -i` reads at most 4096 characters per line and runs the rest as a
# second command, so a longer line would store a truncated value (measured on
# macOS 15/26). Hex doubles the value, which leaves about 1.9KB per item.
SECURITY_LINE_MAX = 4096


def store_command(name, value, typed=False):
    """The `security -i` line. Goes to stdin, never argv. Hex means no quoting
    rules to get wrong, whatever the value contains."""
    if not (valid_name(name) and ACCOUNT_RE.fullmatch(ACCOUNT) and PREFIX_RE.fullmatch(SERVICE_PREFIX)):
        raise KeydropError("bad name or config")
    line = (f"add-generic-password -U -a {ACCOUNT} -s {service(name)} "
            f"-l {service(name) + ('.credential-v1' if typed else '')} -X {value.encode('utf-8').hex()}")
    if len(line) > SECURITY_LINE_MAX:
        raise KeydropError("too long for the keychain")
    return line + "\n"


def keychain_fits(name, value, typed=False):
    try:
        store_command(name, value, typed)
        return True
    except KeydropError:
        return False


def keychain_store(name, value, typed=False):
    """`security -i` does exit with the inner command's status, but only its
    low 8 bits (errSecDuplicateItem, -25299, exits 45), so an OSStatus that is
    a multiple of 256 would exit 0. The exit code alone can't be trusted to
    mean stored: read the item back and compare digests."""
    if not valid_name(name):
        raise KeydropError("bad name")
    r = subprocess.run([SECURITY, "-i"], input=store_command(name, value, typed),
                       capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        # security echoes the failing command name and a code, never the input line.
        raise KeydropError(f"security exited {r.returncode}")
    back = keychain_get(name)
    if back is None or not hmac.compare_digest(
            hashlib.sha256(back.encode("utf-8")).digest(),
            hashlib.sha256(value.encode("utf-8")).digest()):
        raise KeydropError("security exited 0 but the item doesn't read back as stored")
    # The label is the type marker. Check it both ways, so a credential
    # replaced by a single secret can't keep reading back as a credential.
    if keychain_entries().get(name, {}).get("type") != ("credential" if typed else "secret"):
        raise KeydropError("the type marker didn't read back as stored")


def keychain_get(name):
    """`-w` prints non-ASCII values as bare hex, indistinguishable from a value
    that happens to be hex. `-g` marks the two forms apart on stderr:
    `password: "text"` or `password: 0xHEX  "escaped"`."""
    r = subprocess.run([SECURITY, "find-generic-password", "-a", ACCOUNT,
                        "-s", service(name), "-g"], capture_output=True, text=True)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None
    line = next((l for l in r.stderr.splitlines() if l.startswith("password: ")), None)
    if line is None:
        return None
    rest = line[len("password: "):]
    m = re.match(r"0x([0-9A-Fa-f]*)(\s|$)", rest)
    if m:
        return bytes.fromhex(m.group(1)).decode("utf-8", "replace")
    return rest[1:-1] if rest.startswith('"') and rest.endswith('"') else rest


def keychain_entries():
    """{name: {"created": epoch, "updated": epoch, "type": ...}} for every <prefix>* item,
    from dump-keychain's attribute listing. Without -d it prints no secret
    data. cdat survives an -U update and mdat moves with it."""
    r = subprocess.run([SECURITY, "dump-keychain"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        raise KeydropError("Couldn't read the keychain.")
    out, cur = {}, {}

    def flush(item):
        svce = item.get("svce") or ""
        name = svce[len(SERVICE_PREFIX):]
        if item.get("acct") == ACCOUNT and svce.startswith(SERVICE_PREFIX) and valid_name(name):
            out[name] = {"created": item.get("cdat"), "updated": item.get("mdat"),
                         "type": "credential" if item.get("labl") == service(name) + ".credential-v1" else "secret"}

    for line in r.stdout.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("keychain:"):
            flush(cur)
            cur = {}
            continue
        m = re.match(r'\s+"(svce|acct|labl)"<blob>="(.*)"$', line)
        if m:
            cur = dict(cur, **{m.group(1): m.group(2)})
            continue
        # The real tool prints the label by attribute number, not as "labl".
        m = re.match(r'\s+0x00000007 <blob>="(.*)"$', line)
        if m:
            cur = dict(cur, labl=m.group(1))
            continue
        m = re.match(r'\s+"(cdat|mdat)"<timedate>=\S+\s+"(\d{14})Z', line)
        if m:
            cur = dict(cur, **{m.group(1): calendar.timegm(time.strptime(m.group(2), "%Y%m%d%H%M%S"))})
    flush(cur)
    return out


def keychain_list():
    return sorted(keychain_entries())


def keychain_exists(name):
    """Attributes only: without -g or -w, find-generic-password never reads
    the secret, so this can't prompt or put a value in memory."""
    if not valid_name(name):
        raise KeydropError("bad name")
    r = subprocess.run([SECURITY, "find-generic-password", "-a", ACCOUNT, "-s", service(name)],
                       capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30)
    return r.returncode == 0


def keychain_delete(name):
    """Only ever <prefix><NAME_RE> with the configured account: the name is validated
    here, not just by callers, and the service is built from the prefix."""
    if not valid_name(name):
        raise KeydropError("bad name")
    subprocess.run([SECURITY, "delete-generic-password", "-a", ACCOUNT, "-s", service(name)],
                   capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30)
    if keychain_exists(name):
        raise KeydropError("security didn't delete it")


class KeychainVault:
    """The macOS login keychain, behind the SPEC 8.1 vault interface. This is
    the only macOS-specific part of keydrop: pages, handlers and the CLI reach
    the store through STORE, so another store is another class with these
    methods. A typed item arrives already encoded per SPEC 5.4."""
    name = "keychain"

    def capabilities(self):
        return {"native_types": [], "write_channel": "stdin", "lists_without_unlock": True,
                "max_bytes": (SECURITY_LINE_MAX - 160) // 2}

    def fits(self, name, value, typed=False):
        return keychain_fits(name, value, typed)

    def store(self, name, value, typed=False):
        keychain_store(name, value, typed)

    def get(self, name):
        return keychain_get(name)

    def list(self):
        return keychain_entries()

    def exists(self, name):
        return keychain_exists(name)

    def delete(self, name):
        keychain_delete(name)


STORE = KeychainVault()


# ---------- notes ----------

def read_notes():
    try:
        with open(META_FILE) as f:
            data = json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}
    if not isinstance(data, dict):
        return {}
    return {k: v for k, v in data.items() if valid_name(k) and isinstance(v, str)}


def write_note(name, note):
    """note=None drops it. Replaces the file whole, 0600, so a crash leaves
    the old one rather than half a new one."""
    with meta_lock():
        notes = {k: v for k, v in read_notes().items() if k != name}
        if note:
            notes = dict(notes, **{name: note})
        tmp = META_FILE + ".tmp"
        fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, 0o600)
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as f:
            json.dump(notes, f, sort_keys=True)
        os.replace(tmp, META_FILE)


# ---------- tailscale ----------

def ts(*args, timeout=15):
    sock = [f"--socket={TS_SOCKET}"] if TS_SOCKET else []
    return subprocess.run([TAILSCALE, *sock, *args],
                          stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, capture_output=True,
                          text=True, timeout=timeout)


def tailnet_host(plain_http=False, http_hint=True):
    try:
        r = ts("status", "--json")
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
        raise KeydropError(f"tailscale not reachable: {e}")
    if r.returncode != 0:
        raise KeydropError("tailscale not reachable: " + (r.stderr.strip() or r.stdout.strip())[:200])
    st = json.loads(r.stdout)
    if st.get("BackendState") != "Running":
        raise KeydropError(f"tailscale is {st.get('BackendState')}, not Running (is this Mac logged in?)")
    if not plain_http and not st.get("CertDomains"):
        # `serve --https` would block on an "enable Serve" prompt instead of failing.
        raise KeydropError("HTTPS isn't enabled for this tailnet yet (admin console, "
                           "DNS > HTTPS Certificates), so serve can't issue a cert"
                           + (". --http serves plain HTTP inside the tailnet instead"
                              if http_hint else ""))
    return st["Self"]["DNSName"].rstrip(".")


def serve_status():
    """tailscaled's serve config, or None if it can't be read."""
    try:
        r = ts("serve", "status", "--json")
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return None
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None
    try:
        st = json.loads(r.stdout or "{}")
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return st if isinstance(st, dict) else None


def serve_ports_in_use():
    return set(((serve_status() or {}).get("TCP") or {}).keys())


def ledger_route(status, port, entry):
    """True if serve status still has :port exactly as keydrop made it for
    this ledger entry: HTTP or HTTPS (whichever it was) and nothing else, one
    web host, one handler at "/", proxying to http://127.0.0.1:<entry local>."""
    tcp = (status.get("TCP") or {}).get(str(port))
    if not isinstance(tcp, dict) or not isinstance(entry, dict):
        return False
    want = {"HTTP"} if entry.get("plain_http") else {"HTTPS"}
    if set(k for k, v in tcp.items() if v) != want:
        return False
    try:
        return home_route(status, port) == int(entry.get("local"))
    except (AttributeError, TypeError, ValueError):
        return False


def orphan_routes(status, owned, ledger):
    """Split the ledger's unowned entries into (sweep, stale).

    Only routes keydrop recorded creating are ever candidates: the ledger
    (routes.json) gets an entry before `serve` runs and loses it once
    `serve status` shows the route gone. An entry with a live record (owned)
    is skipped. sweep: [(port, plain_http, local)] whose route is still
    exactly as keydrop made it. stale: ports whose route is gone or now
    points somewhere else, so it isn't ours any more; those entries are only
    dropped. A route keydrop has no ledger entry for is never touched, however
    much it looks like one of keydrop's."""
    sweep, stale = [], []
    for port, entry in sorted((ledger or {}).items()):
        if port in owned:
            continue
        if ledger_route(status, port, entry):
            sweep.append((port, bool(entry.get("plain_http")), int(entry["local"])))
        else:
            stale.append(port)
    return sweep, stale


def serve_on(port, local_port, plain_http):
    # Ledgered before `serve` runs: a serve that times out may still write
    # its config, and the sweep can only ever remove what the ledger lists.
    ledger_set(port, {"local": int(local_port), "plain_http": bool(plain_http),
                      "created": time.time()})
    flag = f"--http={port}" if plain_http else f"--https={port}"
    try:
        r = ts("serve", "--bg", "--yes", flag, f"http://127.0.0.1:{local_port}",
               timeout=SERVE_TIMEOUT)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as e:
        out = (e.stdout or b"").decode() if isinstance(e.stdout, bytes) else (e.stdout or "")
        link = re.search(r"https://login\.tailscale\.com/\S+", out)
        raise KeydropError("tailscale serve hung (HTTPS/Serve not enabled on the tailnet?)"
                           + (f" enable at {link.group(0)}" if link else ""))
    if r.returncode != 0:
        raise KeydropError("tailscale serve failed: " + (r.stderr.strip() or r.stdout.strip())[:300])


def route_ours(status, port):
    """True only if serve status was read and still has :port as a single `/`
    proxy to 127.0.0.1:<the same port>, with Funnel off for it. Unreadable
    (None), garbled, gone, repointed or funnelled are all False: fail closed,
    because a page that can't prove serve is claiming its port has to assume
    netstack is forwarding to it, and a funnelled port is open to the internet."""
    if not isinstance(status, dict):
        return False
    try:
        funnel = status.get("AllowFunnel") or {}
        if any(v for k, v in funnel.items() if str(k).rsplit(":", 1)[-1] == str(port)):
            return False
        return home_route(status, port) == int(port)
    except (AttributeError, TypeError, KeyError, ValueError):
        return False


def serve_off(port, plain_http):
    """True only once `serve status` confirms the port is gone. A nonzero exit
    from `off` is fine if the route is gone anyway (already removed); a zero
    exit is not enough if it isn't."""
    flag = f"--http={port}" if plain_http else f"--https={port}"
    try:
        ts("serve", flag, "off", timeout=SERVE_TIMEOUT)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        pass
    st = serve_status()
    gone = st is not None and str(port) not in (st.get("TCP") or {})
    if gone:
        ledger_drop(port)
    return gone


# ---------- state ----------

class serve_lock:
    """Held while a route and its record change together, and while reap
    sweeps, so a sweep can't catch a route between its record being written
    and the route appearing (or the reverse on teardown)."""
    FILE = "serve.lock"

    def __enter__(self):
        self.fd = os.open(os.path.join(STATE_DIR, self.FILE), os.O_RDWR | os.O_CREAT, 0o600)
        fcntl.flock(self.fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        return self

    def __exit__(self, *exc):
        os.close(self.fd)   # releases the flock


class meta_lock(serve_lock):
    FILE = "meta.lock"


class ledger_lock(serve_lock):
    FILE = "routes.lock"


def read_ledger():
    """{port: {"local", "plain_http", "created"}} for every route keydrop
    created and hasn't yet seen gone. Unreadable means empty: the sweep then
    removes nothing, which is the safe direction."""
    try:
        with open(ROUTES_FILE) as f:
            data = json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}
    return {str(k): v for k, v in data.items() if isinstance(v, dict)} if isinstance(data, dict) else {}


def _ledger_update(fn):
    ensure_state()
    with ledger_lock():
        data = fn(read_ledger())
        tmp = ROUTES_FILE + ".tmp"
        fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, 0o600)
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as f:
            json.dump(data, f)
        os.replace(tmp, ROUTES_FILE)


def ledger_set(port, entry):
    _ledger_update(lambda d: dict(d, **{str(port): entry}))


def ledger_drop(port):
    if str(port) in read_ledger():
        _ledger_update(lambda d: {k: v for k, v in d.items() if k != str(port)})


def ensure_state():
    for d in (STATE_DIR, PENDING_DIR):
        os.makedirs(d, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
        os.chmod(d, 0o700)


def log(name, outcome):
    stamp = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z")
    fd = os.open(LOG_FILE, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_APPEND | os.O_CREAT, 0o600)
    with os.fdopen(fd, "a") as f:
        f.write(f"{stamp}\t{name}\t{outcome}\n")


def drop_pending(rec):
    try:
        os.unlink(os.path.join(PENDING_DIR, rec["id"] + ".json"))
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass


def write_pending(rec):
    path = os.path.join(PENDING_DIR, rec["id"] + ".json")
    fd = os.open(path, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, 0o600)
    with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as f:
        json.dump(rec, f)
    return path


def read_pending():
    recs = []
    if not os.path.isdir(PENDING_DIR):
        return recs
    for fn in sorted(os.listdir(PENDING_DIR)):
        if fn.endswith(".json"):
            try:
                with open(os.path.join(PENDING_DIR, fn)) as f:
                    recs.append(json.load(f))
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                continue
    return recs


def alive(pid):
    try:
        os.kill(pid, 0)
    except ProcessLookupError:
        return False
    except PermissionError:
        return True
    r = subprocess.run(["ps", "-p", str(pid), "-o", "command="], capture_output=True, text=True)
    return "keydrop" in r.stdout


def keydrop_listening(local_port):
    """True if a live keydrop process holds 127.0.0.1:local_port, or if that
    can't be determined. Records live in one state dir, but a keydrop running
    with another KEYDROP_STATE_DIR (a test run, a copy of the script) owns its
    route just as much, and the sweep can't see its record."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([shutil.which("lsof") or "/usr/sbin/lsof", "-nP", f"-iTCP@127.0.0.1:{local_port}",
                            "-sTCP:LISTEN", "-t"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return True
    if r.returncode not in (0, 1) or (r.returncode == 1 and r.stderr.strip()):
        return True   # lsof exits 1 with no output when nothing matches
    return any(alive(int(pid)) for pid in r.stdout.split() if pid.isdigit())


def reap():
    """Clean up after a server that died without running its own teardown,
    retry teardowns that failed, and sweep routes the ledger says keydrop made
    that no record owns."""
    with serve_lock():
        for rec in read_pending():
            if not rec.get("teardown_failed") and alive(rec["pid"]):
                continue
            if rec.get("tailnet_port") and not serve_off(rec["tailnet_port"],
                                                         rec.get("plain_http", False)):
                if not rec.get("teardown_failed"):
                    write_pending(dict(rec, teardown_failed=True))
                    log(rec["name"], "teardown-failed")
                continue
            log(rec["name"], "reaped")
            drop_pending(rec)
        # Status before records: a route is only ever created after its record
        # is written, so any route in this snapshot has a record by now.
        st = serve_status()
        if st is None:
            return
        owned = {str(r["tailnet_port"]) for r in read_pending() if r.get("tailnet_port")}
        sweep, stale = orphan_routes(st, owned, read_ledger())
        for port in stale:
            ledger_drop(port)   # gone, or someone else's now: never ours to remove
        for port, plain_http, local in sweep:
            if keydrop_listening(local):
                continue
            log("-", f"swept orphan :{port} " + ("ok" if serve_off(port, plain_http) else "failed"))


